i believe that one area of software-based innovations we should be looking into should be the idea of persuasive computing. currently, there are more bots than humans surfing the internet, and these bots have been trained
on data gathered from humans. and in a sense, humans are naively training these bots to outsource their value in a workplace. there seems to be a common pattern we can identify from solutions which employ a persuasive 
computing approach.

Approach 1: The conventional approach
* the human has a task they wish to complete
* the tool a human wishes to use has an insignificant "toll" fee
* the human values their task so much, that a minor toll fee wouldn't be an inconvience to them
* the human completes their task, the toll is collected, both parties are satisfied
* THE HUMAN MUST ALSO HAVE AT ALL TIMES, FULL VOLUNTARY ABANDONMENT OF THEIR TASK

Appraoch 2: The abusive approach
* the human suffers from an addiction which will be exploited
* the tool the human uses offers a risk-reward based outcome 
* the higher the risk, the more reward, the more we can fuel the human's addiction
* if the human feels at despair, we must fuel them to keep them longer

